# Django coding style

Narrows `python`'s coding style to Django conventions. Applies to `*.py` files inside a Django project; non-Django Python in the same repository follows `python`'s coding-style rule.

## App and project layout

- One Django app per cohesive domain concern, each with its own `models.py`/`views.py`/`urls.py`/`migrations/`.
- Prefix route names with the app namespace (`billing:invoice-detail`) and reverse them with `reverse()`/`{% url %}`, never a hand-built path string.
- Split `settings.py` by environment only when the project already does; match the existing scheme.

## Models

- Give a model field an explicit `verbose_name`/`help_text` when the name alone is not self-explanatory in the admin or a generated form.
- Use `related_name` when the default `<model>_set` would collide or read unclearly; name it from the related side (`author.books`).
- Use `choices=Status.choices` with a `TextChoices`/`IntegerChoices` enum, not a bare tuple of literals repeated per call site.
- Override `save()`/add `clean()` only for invariants that hold for every write path; a rule for one form belongs on that `Form`.

## Views and URLs

- Prefer a class-based view (`ListView`, `DetailView`, `CreateView`, or a DRF `APIView`/`ViewSet`) for standard CRUD shapes; use a function-based view when the logic does not map onto a generic view's hooks. Neither is deprecated.
- Keep business logic out of the view body; delegate to a model method, a form's `clean`/`save`, or a service function.
- Prefer `path()` with converters (`<int:pk>`) over `re_path()` unless the match needs a regex; match the project's existing convention.

## Forms and serializers

- Validate in a `Form`/`ModelForm` (or DRF `Serializer`/`ModelSerializer`) `clean_<field>`/`clean()`/`validate_<field>`/`validate()` method, not ad hoc in the view.
- Use an explicit `fields = [...]` list, never `fields = "__all__"`, on a form or serializer that accepts user-submitted data.

## Imports and structure

- Follow `python`'s import grouping; match the order the project's `ruff`/`isort` config produces rather than hand-ordering.
- Import from `django.conf.settings`; do not re-read environment variables in application code that has a settings value.
